---
description: Directory layout for libs/, src/, assets/ and the dev folders
alwaysApply: true
---

## Layering

- `libs/` is the **systems layer**: reusable, domain-agnostic engines &
  abstractions (movement, items, anim, net). No `src/` references, no
  game-specific names.
- `src/` is the **composed game**: scenes + gameplay scripts that wire `libs/`
  systems together and own game-specific data/content.
- There is no separate "systems" bucket in `src/` — the systems live in `libs/`;
  `src/` only composes them. See `libs/README.md` and `src/README.md`.

## `src/` — broad buckets, feature subfolders

`src/` uses a **small, fixed set of broad, durable buckets**, not file-type
folders and not one folder per feature. New directions are **feature subfolders
inside** these buckets — almost never a new top-level folder.

```
pandemonia/
├── libs/                # Systems & abstractions (see libs/README.md)
├── src/                 # Composed game (see src/README.md)
│   ├── main.tscn        #   single launch scene
│   ├── core/            #   app shell: bootstrap + global autoloads (e.g. Net)
│   ├── actors/          #   anything with agency (player/, npc/, enemies/ …)
│   ├── world/           #   places you play in (levels/ + props/ + _sandbox/)
│   └── ui/              #   menus, HUD
├── assets/              # Raw imports (mirrors src/ buckets)
│   └── shared/          #   cross-bucket only (sparingly)
├── addons/              # Third-party / editor plugins
├── hack/                # Dev-only automation, not shipped (setup, codegen, inspect)
└── tests/               # Headless test scripts (runnable in CI)
```

## `libs/`

- Portable systems in `libs/<system>/` — no game logic, no `src/` references.
- Strategy trio: `@abstract` contract, concrete strategy, `.tres` config (see
  `godot-rules.mdc`).
- `src/` composes libs and owns the game-specific `.tres`.
- Promote to `libs/` only when genuinely reused — don't pre-abstract.

## `src/` buckets

- `core/` — bootstrap + the **global** autoloads/singletons that wire libs
  systems app-wide (one per game). Stays small; most systems are composed at
  their point of use, not here.
- `actors/` — entities with agency; each is a thin composer over `libs/`.
- `world/` — `levels/` (playable scenes), `props/` (placeables/interactables +
  item content), `_sandbox/` (throwaway dev scenes, never shipped).
- `ui/` — menus and HUD.
- Colocate `.tscn` + `.gd` (+ owned `.tres`) per feature; `class_name` only when
  referenced across buckets.

## `assets/`

Path: `assets/<bucket>/<feature>/<type>/<file>` — bucket matches `src/`, feature
is one logical unit, type is a category (`sprites`, `tilesets`, `textures`, …), file is
snake_case.

- Raw imports in `assets/`; composed scenes in `src/` — reference via
  `res://assets/<bucket>/…`.
- No gameplay scripts in `assets/`; colocate scripts with the owning `src/`
  bucket.

## `hack/` and `tests/`

- `hack/` — dev-only automation that is **not the product** (git-hook setup,
  codegen, one-off inspection scripts). Mirrors Go's `hack/` convention.
- `tests/` — headless verification scripts (`SceneTree` runners) meant to be run
  in CI. First-class, kept out of `hack/`.

## Adding a feature / direction

1. Add a **feature subfolder** in the bucket(s) it touches (`actors/<x>/`,
   `world/levels/<x>/`, `world/props/<x>/`, `ui/<x>/`) — not a new top-level dir.
2. Build/extend reusable engines in `libs/<system>/`; compose them in `src/` via
   `.tres`. Add a `core/` autoload only for genuinely app-wide systems.
3. Put raw imports under the matching `assets/<bucket>/<feature>/<type>/`.
4. Commit scope matches the bucket — see `use-convention-commits.mdc`.
